+RTRF_28=The collector adds no listening ports. It only reads local counters (interfaces, conntrack, QoS queues).
+RTRF_29=Collect traffic history
+RTRF_30=Turns the rtrafd collector on or off. Off = the realtime interface monitor still works, but no history or per-device accounting.
+RTRF_31=History storage
+RTRF_32=RAM (fast, cleared on reboot)
+RTRF_33=JFFS (survives reboot, internal flash)
+RTRF_34=USB disk (survives reboot, most space)
+RTRF_35=<b>RAM</b> keeps everything in memory &mdash; the live view and up to a year of rolled-up history, but it all resets when the router reboots. Choose <b>JFFS</b> or <b>USB</b> to keep history across reboots. The collector snapshots once an hour, so at most the last hour is lost on an unclean shutdown. JFFS is limited internal flash (fine for this data, a few hundred KB); USB has the most room and avoids flash wear. If the chosen target is not writable, the collector silently falls back to RAM.
+RTRF_36=WAN latency probe (optional)
+RTRF_37=e.g. 1.1.1.1 (blank = off)
+RTRF_38=A single ping per sample to this host graphs latency and loss under load beside throughput &mdash; the bufferbloat picture. Left blank, nothing is sent (no forced outbound). Use an IP or hostname you are comfortable pinging continuously.
+RTRF_39=GB (0 = off)
+RTRF_40=Apply settings
+RTRF_41=Traffic Analyzer Guide
+RTRF_42=Where is my history stored?
+RTRF_43=Choose in Settings: <b>RAM</b> (default, cleared on reboot), <b>JFFS</b> (internal flash, survives reboot), or <b>USB</b> (a plugged-in disk, most space). Anything longer than the live view needs JFFS or USB if you want it to outlast a reboot. The collector writes a snapshot hourly, so an unclean shutdown loses at most the last hour.
+RTRF_44=How accurate are the per-device numbers?
+RTRF_45=They come from the router's <b>hardware flow accelerator</b> table &mdash; the same engine that forwards your traffic &mdash; so offloaded flows (the bulk of throughput on this router) are counted accurately, not missed. Totals are exact to within a poll interval; a very short flow that opens and closes between polls can undercount slightly. Devices show as a bare IP until the network map names them. IPv6 counts toward the WAN total but is not yet split per-device.
+RTRF_46=Why is the per-class chart empty?
+RTRF_47=Per-class data only exists while <b>HW QoS Classful</b> (Traffic Manager) is the active engine &mdash; that is what creates the five upload queues this reads. On any other QoS mode the class chart stays blank; everything else still works.
+RTRF_48=What are "top talkers"?
+RTRF_49=The highest-rate active connections right now, grouped by destination port so you can tell <i>what kind</i> of traffic it is (HTTPS 443, QUIC 443/UDP, SSH 22, mail, torrent-range, etc.) without deep packet inspection. This is the privacy-respecting replacement for the old per-app view &mdash; it reads only the connection table, never packet contents.
+RTRF_50=How does the data cap work?
+RTRF_51=Set a monthly GB figure and the collector tracks month-to-date WAN usage (up + down). At 80% and again at 100% it writes a warning to the system log; if you have a remote syslog server or SIEM configured, the warning reaches it too. It is a notification, not an enforcement &mdash; nothing is cut off.
+RTRF_52=Does this add any attack surface?
+RTRF_53=No listening ports. The collector only reads local kernel counters and writes JSON files the web UI serves through the normal authenticated session. The optional latency probe is the only outbound traffic, and only to the host you enter.
