+RQOS_01=Quality of Service
+RQOS_02=FAQ / Tuning Guide
+RQOS_03=Pick how upload/download traffic is shaped. Changing the mode (or turning QoS on) reboots the router so the flow accelerator can be reconfigured; edits within a mode apply live.
+RQOS_04=Bandwidth
+RQOS_05=The shaper must own the bottleneck. Set upload to 90&ndash;95% of what a speed test <i>measures</i> (not the plan rate) &mdash; if this equals or exceeds what the line really delivers, the ISP buffer takes over and QoS barely engages.
+RQOS_06=Upload (obw)
+RQOS_07=Download (ibw)
+RQOS_08=Used by Traditional / Bandwidth Limiter / Cake. Hardware QoS shapes upload only.
+RQOS_09=Classes &mdash; priority, guarantees, ceilings
+RQOS_10=Top row is served first (strict priority). <b>Min %</b> is a guaranteed floor under load; <b>Ceiling %</b> is the most a class may ever take. With the aggregate cap on, ceilings may total over 100% &mdash; they become burst allowances inside the capped upload.
+RQOS_100=Ceilings total
+RQOS_101=% and the aggregate cap is OFF: several busy classes together can exceed the upload figure and re-enter the ISP buffer. Continue?
+RQOS_102=No upload bandwidth is set. Hardware QoS will enable AQM but cannot shape without it. Continue anyway?
+RQOS_103=Changing the QoS mode reboots the router so the flow accelerator can be reconfigured. Reboot now?
+RQOS_104=Settings are being applied. This page will refresh.
+RQOS_105=The router is rebooting to switch QoS engines. This page will refresh when it is back.
+RQOS_106=Running:
+RQOS_107=Type
+RQOS_108=Shaping off
+RQOS_109=drops
+RQOS_11=Aggregate cap (port shaper)
+RQOS_12=Holds the whole WAN port at the upload figure above, so combined class traffic can never spill into the ISP buffer &mdash; even when several classes saturate at once. Leave on unless you are debugging.
+RQOS_13=Guaranteed minimums
+RQOS_14=Enforces the Min % column as a committed rate per class. Guarantees only matter under contention; they must total 100% or less.
+RQOS_15=Trust DSCP marking
+RQOS_16=Maps standard DiffServ classes from LAN devices (EF/CS5+ to Q1, AF4x to Q2, AF3x to Q3, CS1 scavenger to Q5) ahead of the default class. Your explicit rules always win. Off by default: any LAN host can influence its own priority when this is on.
+RQOS_17=L4S marking
+RQOS_18=EXPERIMENTAL
+RQOS_19=Flags the class queues L4S: flows that negotiate L4S ECN get congestion <i>marked</i> instead of dropped, holding queueing near zero for capable apps (newer Apple/conference stacks). Harmless if unsupported by a flow &mdash; classic traffic keeps normal PI2 behavior. Unproven on this hardware; watch the drop counters after enabling.
+RQOS_20=Order
+RQOS_21=Class name
+RQOS_22=Queue
+RQOS_23=Weight
+RQOS_24=Min %
+RQOS_25=Ceiling %
+RQOS_26=Rate (live)
+RQOS_27=Drops
+RQOS_28=<b>Priority</b> classes are always served first, top to bottom. <b>Weighted</b> classes form a pool below them that splits the remaining bandwidth by weight (1&ndash;63) instead of rank &mdash; use it when two bulk classes should share fairly rather than starve each other. Weighted classes must sit together at the bottom of the list.
+RQOS_29=Unclassified (Q0)
+RQOS_30=LAN / Multicast (Q6)
+RQOS_31=AQM
+RQOS_32=PI2 on every queue &mdash; drops rise briefly when a class is held to its rate; that is the system working.
+RQOS_33=FAQ and tuning guide
+RQOS_34=Which mode should I pick?
+RQOS_35=<b>HW QoS Classful</b> &mdash; the recommended default. Five priority classes, guaranteed minimums, hardware AQM, and the NAT accelerator stays on (full multi-gigabit routing).
+RQOS_36=<b>Hardware QoS</b> &mdash; single-queue version: one upload cap + AQM, no classes. Simplest possible bufferbloat fix.
+RQOS_37=<b>Cake</b> &mdash; best <i>download</i> bufferbloat control, but it is software: the NAT accelerator turns off and one CPU core does all shaping. Fine below ~1 Gb/s.
+RQOS_38=<b>Traditional</b> &mdash; the classic software engine. Accelerator off. Only if you need its exact semantics.
+RQOS_39=<b>Bandwidth Limiter</b> &mdash; not bufferbloat control; hard per-device speed caps (guest devices, kids' devices).
+RQOS_40=How do I set the bandwidth figure?
+RQOS_41=Run a few speed tests at a quiet hour, take the <b>lowest consistent upload</b>, and enter 90&ndash;95% of it. The point is that <b>this router</b> must be the bottleneck: if the value is at or above what the line truly delivers, queueing happens in the ISP's equipment where nothing here can manage it. Too low just wastes headroom.
+RQOS_42=Priority vs Minimum vs Ceiling &mdash; what does each do?
+RQOS_43=<b>Priority (row order)</b>: under contention the top class is served first, completely. Rank by latency-sensitivity, not importance.<br><br>
+RQOS_44=<b>Min %</b>: a floor the class is guaranteed when the link is saturated. Idle guarantees cost nothing. Mins must total 100% or less &mdash; you cannot promise more than the link.<br><br>
+RQOS_45=<b>Ceiling %</b>: the most the class may take, ever. The ceiling on a <i>high</i>-priority class is what protects everyone below it from starvation &mdash; never give Q1 100%.
+RQOS_46=Suggested setups
+RQOS_47=<b>Work-from-home</b> (calls first):
+RQOS_48=<ul><li>Q1 Web/VoIP &mdash; min 15 / ceil 20</li><li>Q2 Gaming &mdash; min 5 / ceil 10</li><li>Q3 Streaming &mdash; min 10 / ceil 30</li><li>Q4 Default &mdash; min 20 / ceil 50</li><li>Q5 Downloads &mdash; min 10 / ceil 95</li></ul>
+RQOS_49=<b>Gamer</b>: move Gaming to Q1 (min 10 / ceil 15), VoIP Q2.<br>
+RQOS_50=<b>Small office</b>: raise Default's min (most traffic is unclassified there) and keep bulk backup jobs in Q5 with a high ceiling &mdash; bottom priority makes them yield instantly when anyone needs the line.
+RQOS_51=Priority vs Weighted classes &mdash; when to use which?
+RQOS_52=<b>Priority</b> (the default) is absolute rank: under load, a higher class takes everything its ceiling allows before a lower class gets a byte. Perfect for latency tiers.<br><br>
+RQOS_53=<b>Weighted</b> classes opt out of ranking: all weighted classes form one pool <i>below</i> every Priority class, and the pool splits whatever bandwidth is left in proportion to weight (1&ndash;63). Use it when two bulk-ish classes should share fairly instead of one starving the other &mdash; e.g. make Downloads (weight 40) and a Backups class (weight 20) weighted, and backups always get a third of the leftover no matter how greedy downloads are.<br><br>
+RQOS_54=Rule the editor enforces: weighted rows sit together at the bottom &mdash; a Priority row below the pool would silently outrank the whole pool.
+RQOS_55=Can I cap a GROUP of classes together?
+RQOS_56=There is no group-shaper control (the hardware's shaper-object API is not usable from the firmware's tooling), but the same outcome falls out of two existing levers: with the <b>aggregate cap on</b>, give the group members ceilings that <b>sum to the group budget</b> &mdash; e.g. Streaming 30% + Downloads 30% can never exceed 60% combined. Add weights (previous question) if the members should also share that budget fairly.
+RQOS_57=What is L4S and should I turn it on?
+RQOS_58=L4S (Low Latency, Low Loss, Scalable throughput) is a newer congestion-control standard: capable flows get their congestion signaled by <b>marking</b> packets (ECN) instead of dropping them, so the sender backs off within a round-trip and queueing stays near zero. Newer Apple devices and some conferencing/game stacks negotiate it; everything else is unaffected and keeps normal PI2 behavior.<br><br>It is marked <b>experimental</b> here: the queue hardware advertises the capability but it has not been proven on this board. Safe to try &mdash; if it misbehaves (a class suddenly shows zero drops but poor latency), switch it back off and re-apply.
+RQOS_59=Why do bulk classes get a HIGH ceiling?
+RQOS_60=Strict priority already makes the bottom class yield the moment anything above it wants the line. A high ceiling (95%) simply lets backups saturate an otherwise idle link. Low priority + high ceiling is the correct combination for bulk; a low ceiling would only slow it down at 3 a.m. for no one's benefit.
+RQOS_61=What does DSCP trust actually do?
+RQOS_62=IP packets carry a 6-bit DiffServ field many applications set voluntarily (Teams/Zoom mark voice EF, video AF4x). With the toggle on, marked traffic is classified by that field <b>after</b> your explicit rules and <b>before</b> the default class. It is off by default because any LAN device can mark its own packets &mdash; a torrent client claiming EF would ride Q1. Turn it on when you broadly trust the devices on the LAN (typical WFH/office), keep it off on networks with untrusted clients.
+RQOS_63=How do I verify it is working?
+RQOS_64=Saturate the upload (large cloud backup) and watch the live columns: the bulk class rate should pin near its ceiling, <span class="fx">drops on the bulk queue should tick up</span> (PI2 holding it to rate), and a simultaneous speed test or call should stay responsive. If nothing changes, the bandwidth figure is probably above what the line really delivers.
+RQOS_65=Why is hardware QoS upload-only?
+RQOS_66=The hardware traffic manager sits on the WAN <i>egress</i> (upload) side. Download shaping in hardware is per-port on this platform, which cannot express one aggregate policy for the internet link. If download bufferbloat is your main problem, Cake handles both directions &mdash; at the cost of the NAT accelerator (throughput tops out near 1 Gb/s).
